PAGE 2 — WHY RESILIENT FUTURES / THE PROBLEM WE SOLVE 
PAGE HEADLINE 
The operating environment has changed. 
Most strategic systems haven't. 
This is not a crisis of intent. Most leadership teams are working hard, making 
reasonable decisions, and executing against plans they believe in. The problem 
is structural: the methods, rhythms, and assumptions underpinning most 
strategies were built for a world of relative predictability — and that world is 
gone. 
The strategic reality facing leaders today 
Change is no longer an event to be managed. It is the permanent operating condition. The forces 
reshaping organisations — from AI adoption and workforce transformation, to geopolitical instability, 
shifting member expectations, regulatory pressure, and climate-related disruption — are not sequential. 
They interact. They amplify each other. And they do not pause for annual planning cycles. 
The implications for leaders are profound. 
• Traditional strategy cycles are too slow and 
too narrow for the pace of change 
• Risk registers seldom capture the dynamic 
interdependencies between forces 
• Strategy becomes a 'project list' — 
disconnected from real conditions 
• Boards lack real-time strategic intelligence 
for effective governance 
• Undetected signals of change lead to 
decision lag and missed opportunity 
• Organisations invest in optimising the 
present rather than preparing for the future 
• Leadership effectiveness declines under the 
pressure of disruptive change 
• Capability gaps — in people, systems, and 
infrastructure — quietly widen 
The consequence has a name: Managed Adaptive 
Decline. 
When organisations adapt to deteriorating conditions in a seemingly rational way — cutting costs, 
tightening focus, incrementally adjusting — they can maintain the appearance of function while steadily 
losing relevance, influence, and value. This is what we call Managed Adaptive Decline, or MAD. 
MAD is insidious precisely because it is gradual. Performance metrics may hold. Board confidence may 
remain high. But the organisation is slowly becoming less capable of generating the value that made it 
important in the first place. 
The most significant risk is not the disruption you can see. It is the erosion of 
relevance that is happening too slowly to trigger an alarm. 
Why traditional approaches fall short 
LEAN programs, agile methodologies, design thinking, large-scale restructuring — these tools have 
genuine value in the right contexts. But they are optimisation tools, not orientation tools. They improve 
how an organisation executes within a given frame. They do not help an organisation determine 
whether the frame itself is still the right one. 
What leaders in complex environments need is a fundamentally different approach to strategy — one 
that starts with an honest reading of the real change landscape, moves to where value must be 
generated and protected, identifies the risks and opportunities that matter most, and translates all of 
this into focused, adaptive action. 
There is a better way. 
Resilient Futures works with leaders who have recognised that the current approach to strategy is not fit 
for the conditions they are operating in — and who want to do something about it before the gap 
between strategy and reality becomes a crisis. 
Our approach does not begin with frameworks. It begins with a clear-eyed diagnosis of what is actually 
happening in your operating environment — and what that means for your organisation's relevance, risk 
exposure, and opportunity set. 
Start with a Strategic Fitness conversation. 
We work with a small number of organisations at any given time. A short conversation helps 
determine whether the timing and fit are right for your organisation. →  Book a conversation 
  
PAGE 3 — OUR APPROACH / STRATEGY IN ACTION (SIA) 
PAGE HEADLINE 
Strategy in Action (SiA) A decision
making system built for structural 
instability. 
Most strategy frameworks were designed to produce a plan. Strategy in Action 
was designed to keep strategy alive — continuously connected to the real change 
landscape, continuously generating the insight and alignment your 
organisation needs to act with confidence. 
The thinking behind the methodology 
Strategy in Action (SiA) is grounded in three intellectual traditions that most strategic advisory practices 
do not draw on: complex adaptive systems thinking, resilience science, and cognitive science. 
This is not academic positioning. Each of these disciplines has direct, practical implications for how 
organisations should approach strategy in environments where conditions are unpredictable, 
interdependent, and fast-moving. Complex systems do not behave like machines — they adapt, self
organise, and produce emergent behaviour that no single actor can fully control or predict. 
The implication is significant: strategy in complex environments cannot be a static document. It must be 
a living system — one that helps the organisation read change, test assumptions, and act with both 
speed and judgement. 
The five interconnected concepts 
SiA operates through five standards that work dynamically — not as sequential steps, but as an 
integrated system that keeps strategy aligned to changing conditions at every level of the organisation. 
1. Conditions — Understanding the real change landscape 
Every SiA engagement begins with building a shared, evidence-based understanding of the conditions 
shaping an organisation's operating environment. This goes well beyond scanning headlines or listing 
trends. The intent is to 'read the real play' — to understand how forces are interacting, where pressure 
is building, and what this means specifically for your organisation's relevance, risk exposure, and value 
creation potential. 
This shared view of reality — kept continuously current — becomes the anchor for all subsequent 
strategic decisions. 
2. Sustainable Value Generation — What must endure 
Based on the conditions analysis, we focus on the value the organisation must generate, protect, refine, 
and renew over time. This is not about financial metrics alone. It is about understanding what is 
genuinely valued by members, stakeholders, partners, and the broader system — and where the 
organisation must differentiate to remain relevant and trusted. 
This sharpens strategic judgement by answering the hardest question: what is this organisation actually 
for, in the conditions we now face? 
3. Strategic Opportunity-Risk (SOR) — Focus where it matters 
In complex environments, opportunity and risk are frequently two sides of the same coin. SiA identifies 
and prioritises Strategic Opportunity-Risks — the conditions, choices, and dynamics that have the most 
significant implications for the organisation's trajectory. 
This provides clear strategic focus: a disciplined view of what initiatives should continue, adapt, 
accelerate, or stop, structured across near, medium, and longer-term horizons. 
4. Capability Investment — Matching ambition with readiness 
Capability is the engine of sustainable value generation. SiA creates a deliberate, networked view of the 
capabilities an organisation must build, acquire, release, or repurpose — in people, systems, 
infrastructure, and operating models — to remain effective as conditions evolve. 
This is where strategy stops being aspirational and becomes honest about what the organisation can 
and cannot do. 
5. Catalytic Action — Strategy that moves 
SiA translates strategy into targeted catalytic action: initiatives with multi-pronged impact that generate 
feedback quickly and determine next action — all moving in flow with the pace of change. Rather than 
large, slow, linear programs, this approach emphasises fast learning cycles and adaptive execution. 
As a complete framework, SiA works at all levels of the organisation and across silos. It 
provides a common strategic language, supports genuine empowerment of people, and 
fosters accountability to action — not just to plans. 
What makes this fundamentally different 
Most strategic advisory practices do the thinking for you — and leave when the document is done. 
Resilient Futures does something more important and more enduring: we transfer the mindset, tools, 
and behaviours required to think and act strategically to your people, so your organisation builds 
genuine internal capability over time. 
This is not about outsourcing strategic thinking. It is about building it into the fabric of how your Board 
and leadership team work. 
Traditional strategy consulting 
• Produces a plan 
• Framework is proprietary to the consultant 
• Engagement ends at delivery 
• Juniors do the work 
• Board sees polished outputs 
Strategy in Action (SiA) 
• Builds a living strategy system 
• Framework is transferred to your people 
• Engagement builds lasting internal capability 
• Senior practitioners only — your investment 
trains your team, not ours 
• Board engages directly with real strategic 
intelligence 
How we get started 
Every engagement begins with a short Strategic Fitness conversation to understand your context and 
determine whether the timing and fit are right. From there, a pathway is co-designed — shaped by your 
organisation's specific challenges, priorities, and pace. 
Our typical starting points include the Strategic Fitness diagnostic workshop, a SiA Kick-Start session, or 
a full strategy facilitation engagement. All are designed as proof-of-value experiences that demonstrate 
how we work before any longer-term commitment is made. For organisations that wish to go deeper, 
the SiA Practice Community provides an online platform for ongoing learning and practice — but this is 
an option for those who want it, not a requirement of engagement. 
Explore whether SiA is right for your organisation. 
Book a Strategic Fitness conversation — a short, no-obligation discussion that helps both of us 
determine whether there is a genuine fit. →  Book a conversation 
PAGE 4 — OUR TEAM 
PAGE HEADLINE 
The people behind the work. 
Resilient Futures is a small, senior team of seasoned strategic change and 
transformation practitioners. When you engage us, you work directly with the 
people who bring the depth — not with the juniors learning on your investment. 
Larry Quick — Co-founder & Lead Practitioner 
Larry Quick has spent over 25 years working at the intersection of strategy, complex systems, and 
organisational transformation. His work has taken him across Australia, the United States, and 
internationally — engaging with government, industry, health, education, and professional association 
sectors at the highest levels. 
Larry's thinking on strategy for disruptive change is grounded in complex adaptive systems theory and 
resilience science — disciplines that informed the development of Strategy in Action long before 
'disruption' became a consulting industry buzzword. He co-authored Disrupted: Strategy for Exponential 
Change (2015), widely regarded as ahead of its time in its analysis of converging change forces and their 
strategic implications. 
His direct experience spans economic development strategy for the State of Rhode Island, community 
resilience programs across the United States, peak body and professional association strategy in 
Australia, corporate transformation in manufacturing and higher education, and Board-level advisory 
work with health networks and government agencies. 
Larry brings the rare combination of intellectual rigour, practitioner credibility, and the capacity to ask 
the questions that most advisors are not willing to ask. 
David Platt — Co-founder & Lead Practitioner 
David Platt brings deep expertise in organisational transformation, capability development, and the 
human dimensions of strategic change. With a career spanning complex change programs in 
manufacturing, professional associations, health services, and multi-stakeholder environments, David 
brings both the strategic and the practical to every engagement. 
He co-authored Disrupted: Strategy for Exponential Change alongside Larry Quick, and has spent over 
two decades developing and refining the facilitation approaches, learning systems, and capability 
transfer methodologies that make Strategy in Action practical and enduring for client organisations. 
David's particular focus is on what it takes for leadership teams to genuinely think and act differently — 
not just to understand a new framework, but to build the mindset, behaviours, and habits that make 
adaptive strategy a natural part of how an organisation operates. 
We're not a traditional consultancy. Our role is to work alongside your leadership team 
— bringing the thinking, challenging the assumptions, and transferring the tools — so 
that your organisation builds real strategic capability over time. Not dependency. 
How we work 
Resilient Futures operates as a fully distributed, hybrid practice. We deliver engagements in person and 
online, synchronously and asynchronously — through what we call RF3D Collaboration: Dynamic 
Dialogue and Documentation. 
This approach reflects both a principled commitment to minimising unnecessary travel and a genuine 
capability advantage: our cloud-based virtual project environment means that analysis, synthesis, and 
strategic dialogue are available continuously to all participants — not just during workshop days. 
It also means that participants who cannot attend scheduled sessions do not fall behind. They can 
engage asynchronously, at their own pace, and re-enter the program flow without disruption. 
Want to understand how we work before committing? 
A short Strategic Fitness conversation is the best place to start. You'll get a clear sense of who 
we are, how we think, and whether the fit is right. →  Book a conversation with the team 
